CREATED-BY: DXLog.net v2.6.24
SOAPBOX: In recent years, we have pretty much always participated in our Favorite
SOAPBOX: CQ160 contest, and this year was not different. Its CW part was held last
SOAPBOX: weekend.
SOAPBOX: A week before the CQ 160, we were diligently building antennas
SOAPBOX: throughout all weekend. On Saturday, the weather was not good,
SOAPBOX: foggy with strong wind in gusts. It really was not very pleasant to run
SOAPBOX: outside in the meadow, but we still managed to build everything on
SOAPBOX: Saturday. On Sunday we only stretched the coaxial cables, tuned
SOAPBOX: the vertical and completed all the detailed preparation for the test.
SOAPBOX: The transmitting antenna was a vertical 27m with a capacitive hat and
SOAPBOX: several beverage 320m of length. Double beverage for W/VE direction,
SOAPBOX: single for JA and Indian ocean directions, and bidirectional for PY/VK
SOAPBOX: and Pacific/Africa directions. During the test we have realized
SOAPBOX: something wrong on BID BEV AF/NORTH.....In completely dark with
SOAPBOX: headlamp me and Milan OK1IW have fixed the wrong Back-Back on
SOAPBOX: 75coax...That is pretty much “the weak” point on it..
SOAPBOX: For the first time, we had the opportunity to try out the YAESU
SOAPBOX: FTDX10 and FTDX101 radios. It was a big step forward in quality.
SOAPBOX: We used to use an ICOM 756PROIII and the transformation to the new
SOAPBOX: digital device was very nice.
SOAPBOX: On the Monday before the race, a strong "solar wind" hit the Earth and
SOAPBOX: a strong aurora borealis occurred. This phenomenon always has a
SOAPBOX: negative impact on the conditions of propagation of HF signals. We
SOAPBOX: feared the worst, the 160m zone would not work.
SOAPBOX: On the Friday before the race, we met together, made the last
SOAPBOX: preparations and in the evening at 22:00 UTC kicked it out. In the first
SOAPBOX: night, there were 566 Qs in the log, of which over 50 DXs were
SOAPBOX: W/VE/Carib/SA. We expected it to be much, much worse. The second
SOAPBOX: night was miserable on DXs. We saw hundreds of spots in the
SOAPBOX: DX-cluster, where American stations spotted each other. Almost none
SOAPBOX: of this was heard by us even on our quiet double US beverage from
SOAPBOX: what we heard, we made half of stations on Band map. On Sunday
SOAPBOX: evening we were able to do only one JN1ILK of big surprise and
SOAPBOX: surprisingly, VK2BJ with super nice signal on my CQ Freq. I could not
SOAPBOX: understand to work a Barry on the PY dir. That always make a BIG
SOAPBOX: surprise and shaking my head....
SOAPBOX: A big thank you to everyone who participated in the event. We are
SOAPBOX: looking forward to the SSB part. We would also like to thank the
SOAPBOX: Hodkovice nad Mohelkou Aeroclub, which has been accommodating us
SOAPBOX: for many years and providing us with space for these sessions.
SOAPBOX: SETUP: FTDX10 MULTI, FTDX101 RUN plus PA.
SOAPBOX: TX ANT: 27m VERTICAL with capacitive hat plus 100 pcs ¼ radials
SOAPBOX: RX: 320m double US BEV, Middle East SINGLE BEV, PY/VK BIDI,
SOAPBOX: AF/PAC BIDI.
SOAPBOX: Ops: OK1NY, OK1MV, OK1HGM, OK1IW Support TEAM: OK1FJX, OK1DIU,
SOAPBOX: OK2MTW, OK1HGM Jun, OK1FPD
SOAPBOX: See you in SSB part on March.
SOAPBOX: 73. Take Care! MV
